    <statement><p>Given 
<m>z=f(x,y),\quad x=x(u,v),\quad y=y(u,v)</m>,
with
<m>x(3,5) = 5</m> and <m>y(3,5) = 4</m>, calculate
<m>z_v(3,5)</m> in terms of
some of the values given in the table below.</p>
<tabular bottom="medium" left="medium" right="medium" top="medium">
<row>
<cell><m>f_x(3,5) = c</m></cell>
<cell><m>f_y(3,5) = s</m></cell>
<cell><m>x_u(3,5) = -4</m></cell>
<cell><m>y_u(3,5) = b</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell><m>f_x(5,4) = 2</m></cell>
<cell><m>f_y(5,4) = 2</m></cell>
<cell><m>x_v(3,5) = p</m></cell>
<cell><m>y_v(3,5) = q</m></cell>
</row>
</tabular>
<p><m>z_v(3,5) =</m> <fillin characters="45" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>2p+2q</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><me>z_u(u,v) = f_x(x,y)\cdot x_v(u,v) + f_y(x,y)\cdot y_v(u,v).</me>
Since <m>x(3,5)=5</m> and <m>y(3,5)=4</m>, substituting gives
<me>z_u(3,5) = f_x(5,4)\cdot x_v(3,5) + f_y(5,4)\cdot y_v(3,5) = 2p+2q.</me>



</solution>
